Standard_StackTrace: no <execinfo.h> and no backtrace() on wasm32-wasip1.

EXTENDS the two conditions already there, one token each, and adds no new structure:

  * the include chain's `#elif !defined(_WIN32) && !(defined(TARGET_OS_IPHONE) && TARGET_OS_IPHONE)`
    gains `&& !defined(__wasi__)`, so WASI stops selecting <execinfo.h>. No arm then matches and
    nothing is included, which is exactly how that chain already handles iOS.
  * the body chain's `#elif defined(OCCT_UWP) || (defined(TARGET_OS_IPHONE) && TARGET_OS_IPHONE)`
    gains `|| defined(__wasi__)`. That arm already exists and already says the right thing for a
    platform with no backtrace.

WHAT THE WASI BRANCH RETURNS: Standard::StackTrace() sends
"Standard::StackTrace() is not implemented for this platform" as a Message_Trace and returns false,
leaving theBuffer as the caller passed it. That is the branch OCCT_UWP, iOS, Android and QNX
already take. Every in-tree caller is safe with it:

  * Standard_Failure.cxx:87 appends the buffer only inside `if (Standard::StackTrace(...))`, so a
    false return leaves the failure message exactly as it would be with
    OSD::SetSignalStackTraceLength(0), which is the default.
  * OSD_signal.cxx's two handlers memset their buffer before the call and pass it on whatever the
    result, so an untouched buffer is an empty string. Neither is reachable on WASI in any case:
    wasi-osd-signal.patch installs no handler there.

Nothing in the tree parses the buffer or asserts on a frame count.

Issue: https://github.com/SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift/issues/2173

